6|0@relationships^1440764923#cuijgvz:jessislol$:I can't give you direct advice but this happened to me. My SO of 7 years emotionally cheated with a girl from work, he was dealing with some mental health issues and stupidly I didn't realise he was getting closer to her emotionally (I passed it off trying to be the 'cool girlfriend' who lets her partner have friends that are girls). Short story: he broke up with me after having a meltdown, there were so many discussions for weeks afterwards, he thought the other girl somewhat 'liked' him but when she told him she didn't (she is basically one of those who gets their kicks from drama/leading people on) he was able to reassess everything after seeing a psychologist. Basically he had over sensationalised what he thought this girl was like (a bit like Paper Towns, if you've read it). Eventually I took him back and it's been great. Definitely takes a lot to feel normal after what he did to me and it hasn't been an easy road. I still can't let it go completely and although I know his mental health is back on track and that our relationship is strong the scars will always be there. I have asked him if he worries that I may do the same to him and he says I love him too much and that we are the best we've ever been. If you are still feeling so much guilt, please see a couples counsellor or one by yourself, it really can make a difference. All the best.
