44|0@relationships^1440765025#cuijidd:grasmat$:I wish I knew what it's called, but there's this psychological phenomenon where people who do things that are considered 'bad' (stealing, cheating, etc.) are more likely to think other people do the same things ("I've done it, so other people will do it as well") than people who do not do these things ("I don't do it because it's morally wrong, so I can't see why it would be an appealing thing for other people to do"). This seems to be what is happening to you in combination with the guilt you feel (you say Jane and you are fine now, but is all of this *really* in the past for her or is she still hurting or feeling resentment that you pick up on that is 'feeding' the guilt?). You cheated, you know it's a shitty thing to do, and now you're afraid she will do the same either out of retalliation or because "you did it, so why wouldn't she?". You should look into therapy, since the way you are feeling right now is not okay for you and it's also not fair to Jane, since she doesn't deserve these feelings of distrust.
